items: v3-flatmap-nonlist-lane-divergence
paths: file:v3/src/Exec.scala file:v3/BUGS.md file:v3/parity-gate.sh file:v3/tests/parity
scope: |
  The owner asked (2026-08-14) for `v3-multishot-handler-without-a-return-clause` to be fixed. THE
  ENTRY I FILED THIS MORNING NAMES THE WRONG MECHANISM and this claim corrects it as well as the
  code: nothing about multi-shot handlers is wrong. The defect is `flatMap` (and `++`) on a NON-LIST
  result, and the effect fixture is merely the loudest place it shows.

  MEASURED, NOT REASONED, and the table is the whole case:

      op                     reference (bin/ssc run)   v2 VM (v3 --bridge)   v3 exec
      List.flatMap non-list  List(10, 20, 30)          List(10, 20, 30)      REFUSES
      List ++ non-list       List(1, 2, 5)             -                     REFUSES
      List.zip non-list      refuses "expected a list" -                     refuses

  A non-list is ONE ELEMENT on every lane that answers. `zip` already agrees, so it stays as it is.

  THIS IS A LIVE I-3 VIOLATION AND THAT IS THE STRONGEST FACT HERE: `v3/ssc3 run --bridge` prints
  `List(10, 20, 30)` for a program `v3/ssc3 run` REFUSES. The two lanes of one compiler disagree,
  which invariant I-3 exists to forbid — not a v3-vs-reference argument I could be talked out of.

  IT REVERSES PART OF AN OWNER-DIRECTED DECISION, so the reason has to be better than a preference.
  `7730f6039` (2026-08-12) changed `listOut` from SWALLOWING a non-list to REFUSING it, on the
  owner's instruction and with a real measurement behind it, and it was right that the swallow was
  wrong: a swallowed element made `xs.flatMap(f)` produce the empty list and a `foldLeft` over that
  returned a number that looked like an answer. What that commit got wrong is one sentence — "v2's
  runtime still swallows". IT DOES NOT: v2 WRAPS, measured today on v3's own bridge lane, and
  `git log -S flatMap 4a93c440c..HEAD -- v2/src/` is empty, so it wrapped then too. Swallow, wrap
  and refuse are THREE behaviours and the commit collapsed two of them. The corpus agrees with wrap
  independently: `js-effect-multishot-long-fold` has a checked-in expectation of 204, which is what
  a wrapping lane produces and what v3 answered 0 against.

  THE FIX IS NOT IN THE SHARED HELPER. `listOut` serves `flatMap`, `zip` and `++`, and the three do
  not want the same answer — `zip` refuses on every lane. So a wrapping variant is used at the
  `flatMap` and `++` sites (Exec.scala:1339 and :1412) and `zip` (:1354) keeps `listOut`. Putting it
  in `listOut` would silently change `zip` to agree with nobody.

  MEASUREMENT: N with floors DIFF 3 and CRASH 9 — and this one is EXPECTED to move, unlike the last
  four changes: `7730f6039` recorded its own price as CRASH 3 -> 4 and LANE-EXCL 2 -> 1, so undoing
  the refusal in favour of the correct answer should give some of that back. `bench-corpus-gate`
  must go from 33 to 34 of 36 rows, and `js-effect-multishot-long-fold` must answer its checked-in
  204 rather than 0 — that last one is the check that distinguishes WRAP from the old SWALLOW, since
  swallowing also removes the refusal while still answering 0.
next: wrap at the two sites, prove 204, re-run bench-corpus-gate, then rewrite the BUGS entry
